# Decko TypeScript port: source package

This is an independent TypeScript port of Decko, offered under
GPL-3.0-or-later; see NOTICE and LICENSE for attribution and terms. It is not
an upstream Decko release. It targets card 1.110.0 and decko 0.20.0. The
reference runtime for comparisons is the original Ruby Decko of those versions
on Ruby 3.2.3 on the project's Linux VM, where the synthetic-v1 recorded Ruby
outputs were captured on 2026-11-03; EVIDENCE.md states the reference each
quoted result was measured against.

**Package source:** HANDOVER-MANIFEST.json and RUBY-MAP.md are generated when
the package is exported, by the project's export script, which is not part
of this package. The recipient repository's own commit identifies this
source. The manifest's
sourceCommit names the development commit the package was exported from, and
engineCommit names the engine source commit. Those fields identify source
only; they do not report a passing build, smoke check or comparison.

**What was compared:** complete synthetic-v1 grades pass on measured Node/SQLite
and native MySQL 8.4.11 development snapshots against stock Ruby 3.2.3 answers:
rendering 1754/1754, semantics 2389/2389, persistence 16/16 and side effects
19/19. The renamed synthetic-v2 fixture also passes its complete comparison:
2557 matching render results and 24 correctly reproduced Ruby errors across
2581 operations. Those 24 are matching failures of the application, not
remaining comparison mismatches. Main-page and bare-Date error handling have
been corrected; no fixture or comparison rule was changed to hide them.

The complete mod-write probe still fails: its latest recorded checkpoint has
278 persistence mismatches, 303 side-effect mismatches and 275 other-table
mismatches. Those failures require fixes before full mod-write compatibility
or release readiness is claimed. Passing rendering does not close that work.
[EVIDENCE.md](EVIDENCE.md) names each measured source, runtime, fixture and
result, including historical browser and database checks. A result for one
development snapshot is not a grade of a new combined source or exported
package. The earlier notification-link failure and its correction remain in
[KNOWN-COMPARISON-FAILURE.md](KNOWN-COMPARISON-FAILURE.md).

**Library source:** the complete official TinyMCE 5.10.7 source tree
accompanies its minified library under third-party-source/tinymce-5.10.7/.
Its provenance record pins the official tag commit and every source file.

## Build from an empty working directory

Prerequisites: Node.js 22.12 or later (the package.json engines field; tested
on Node 22.23.3) and npm. Dependency versions and integrity values are
pinned by package-lock.json. No cloud account or credentials are required.
Each new export needs its own recorded build. Earlier exports were built with the package's own
commands, below, on 3 November 2026 on a Linux ARM64 machine (Ubuntu, Node
22.23.3, npm 10.9.9): npm ci, the engine build and the smoke test passed, and
every file the manifest marks executable was executable. Those exports were
made from earlier source than this package, so those builds are not a build
of this package. The commands are:

```sh
npm ci --ignore-scripts
npm run build:engine
npm test
```

The build compiles src/ to dist/src/, verifies the static minifier, copies
vendor/ into dist/vendor/, and imports the actual renderer and compiler.
Other platforms and dependency sets need their own check. The build writes
its record to .runtime/browser-build.json. Its provenance helper accepts the
export-generated HANDOVER-MANIFEST.json in place of a Git checkout.

[EVIDENCE.md](EVIDENCE.md) states the runtime and configuration of each quoted
result, and the machine wherever its run records name one.

`npm test` is the package smoke check: it opens the included synthetic deck,
renders a card and checks the license and runtime assets. The redistributable
development tests and their reference inputs also ship; `npm run test:suite`
runs the included Vitest suite. Development-only tooling tests and cases with
unavailable or unproven inputs are excluded as described in
[UNTESTED-INPUTS.md](UNTESTED-INPUTS.md). Shipping tests is not a claim that a
complete suite has passed on this export, or that hosted runtime support and
migration acceptance are complete.

## Run locally

Prepare a disposable copy of the synthetic deck and its files. This also
applies the data-version migration the renderer needs. Start the Node host
with the copy's path and an unused port:

```sh
node tools/handover/prepare-demo.mjs
node vm-staging-server.mjs ./tmp/demo/deck.sqlite3 3100
```

These commands are intended for a loopback host. The host's health check
(GET /_status), Home page (GET /) and graceful shutdown (POST /_shutdown)
have not been checked against this package. A check of an earlier version of
the host through those same HTTP requests does not establish them for this
source. Never point this local host at a production database.
The preparation command copies the synthetic deck's files beside its database
and refuses to overwrite an existing demo. Hosting and deployment
configuration are outside this package; no command above creates cloud
resources.

The settings a deployment of the port needs when it is compared with a
running Ruby Decko site, and why Ruby's view cache is turned off for that
comparison, are recorded in [DEPLOYMENT.md](DEPLOYMENT.md).
Unavailable test inputs and their unverified coverage are listed in
[UNTESTED-INPUTS.md](UNTESTED-INPUTS.md), which separates shipped tests,
unavailable inputs, historical suite results and the remaining write failures.

`node tools/handover/verify-local-host.mjs` is the local-check helper. It has
not been run against this package, so its compatibility with this host and its
cleanup of child processes are untested.

## Local migration and recovery

Importer and recovery source is retained under tools/migrate/. Read
docs/handover/MIGRATION.md and docs/migration-recovery.md before using them.
Recovery operates on isolated SQLite copies and upload trees, refuses existing
restore destinations and preserves the failed target for reconciliation.
Database and file writers must be paused for the snapshot; this is not an
atomic live backup across both stores. Private inputs and their generated
snapshots must not be added to this package.

Redistributable migration tests are included; tests needing private inputs or
development-only tools remain excluded. Local synthetic checks do not establish
a complete private-deck migration, original-user-password access, hosted
providers, real attachments or a cutover. The tests that need private or
absent inputs, the two untested trial-database checks and the hosted
comparison that real-data behaviour rests on (a Ruby Decko site used as the
comparison reference, against a hosted deployment of the port on the Bunny
(bunny.net) edge platform) are listed in
[UNTESTED-INPUTS.md](UNTESTED-INPUTS.md).

## Reproduce comparisons

The package includes the reference harness and synthetic-v1, synthetic-v2,
synthetic-v2-mod-probes, synthetic-v3-mod-probes-ruby33 and write-battery-v1
reference inputs. Each fixture's manifest states its recorded-output coverage;
the v3 directory name is historical, and its new Ruby 3.2.3 evidence covers
rendering only. After a successful build, the diagnostic comparison
commands are:

```sh
node reference-harness/runner/run.mjs --adapter reference-harness/adapters/ts/adapter.json --fixture reference-harness/fixtures/synthetic-v1 --out tmp/reference-run
node reference-harness/runner/grade.mjs --fixture reference-harness/fixtures/synthetic-v1 --actual tmp/reference-run --diagnostic
```

This command requests a diagnostic comparison explicitly; its verdict is
diagnostic, never a full pass. A full grade needs a Git checkout and the exact
full commit being graded, given to the grader with `--candidate-commit`. Read
the raw report for rendering, semantics, persistence and side effects
separately, including failures and skipped operations. Comparison rules are
in reference-harness/SPEC.md and reference-harness/NORMALIZATION-EVIDENCE.md.
Captured expectations are evidence for their fixed synthetic deck, not all
existing decks.
Private captures, four captures without proven input origins and their three
runtime test readers stay out of the export. A runner naming missing steps is
reporting unexecuted coverage, not a successful check. Do not distribute
unproven inputs to make an excluded test appear runnable.

## Review order and package contents

Read PORT-SCOPE.md, DIFFERENCES.md, RUBY-MAP.md (generated at export), then
source and NOTICE. HANDOVER-MANIFEST.json inventories every packaged file with
its original Git mode, byte size and SHA-256. Mode metadata recorded on
Windows does not establish native POSIX permission preservation.
The recipient repository's own commit identifies this source. The
manifest's sourceCommit names the development commit the package was exported
from, and engineCommit names the engine source commit; sourceCommit and
engineCommit may be the same commit. The development commit history is not
included; it was not rewritten, only left out. The export runs a pattern
scanner for private paths and secrets; a pattern check is not a guarantee
that arbitrary secrets or real-deck data are absent.
